("en_US", "LOC_PEDIA_CIVILIZATIONS_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GEDEMO_ZIMBABWE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "Proto-Shona speaking societies first emerged in the middle Limpopo valley in the 9th century before moving on to the Zimbabwean highlands. The Zimbabwean plateau eventually became the centre of subsequent Shona states, beginning around the 10th century. Around the early 10th century, trade developed with Arab merchants on the Indian Ocean coast, helping to develop the Kingdom of Mapungubwe in the 11th century. This was the precursor to the more impressive Shona civilisations that would dominate the region during the 13th to 15th centuries. Having been invaded multiple times, experiencing mass migration from the southern Zululands, and finally British Annexation, the Zimbabwe of today is far detached from that in antiquity. If anything has survived, it is the traditional arts. Shona sculpture has become world famous in recent years having first emerged in the 1940s. Most subjects of carved figures of stylised birds and human figures among others are made with sedimentary rocks such as soapstone, as well as harder igneous rocks such as serpentine and the rare stone verdite. Some of these Zimbabwean artefacts are being found in countries like Singapore, China and Canada, indicating the true extent of the ancient trade links."),
("zh_Hans_CN", "LOC_PEDIA_CIVILIZATIONS_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GEDEMO_ZIMBABWE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "原始修纳语社会于9世纪首先出现在林波波河谷中部，然后转移到津巴布韦高原。津巴布韦高原最终成为后来修纳族国家的中心，大约从10世纪开始。大约在10世纪初，与印度洋沿岸的阿拉伯商人的贸易得到发展，帮助在11世纪发展了马彭古布韦王国。这是13至15世纪期间主宰该地区的更令人印象深刻的修纳文明的前身。由于多次被入侵，经历了来自南部祖鲁兰地区的大规模移民，最后被英国吞并，今天的津巴布韦已经与古代的津巴布韦相差甚远。如果有什么东西幸存下来，那就是传统艺术。修纳人的雕塑近年来已成为世界著名的作品，在1940年代首次出现。大多数雕刻的主题都是用沉积岩，如皂石，以及更硬的火成岩，如蛇纹石和罕见石不纯铬云母石。其中一些津巴布韦的工艺品在新加坡、中国和加拿大等国家被发现，表明了古代贸易联系的真实程度。"),
